  <string key="temp_help_open">Why is the CPU temperature missing?</string>
  <string key="temp_help_title">Why the CPU temperature is missing</string>
  <string key="temp_help_why">No source answered, so the dashboard has no number to show. It does not measure the temperature itself and it starts nothing: it reads what a program that does measure it publishes. Install one of these and leave it running - no add-on, no plug-in, nothing to install inside the dashboard:</string>
  <string key="temp_help_ct_hint">the lightest, one reading per core</string>
  <string key="temp_help_lhm_hint">successor of Open Hardware Monitor</string>
  <string key="temp_help_ohm_hint">the older, still used</string>
  <string key="temp_help_hwinfo_hint">wants its WMI provider switched on</string>
  <string key="temp_help_bios">Do not count on the BIOS or on ACPI: the BIOS almost never exposes the temperature of the CPU, and the ACPI thermal zone is a zone of the motherboard - often the chassis - not the processor. Those programs read the CPU itself, which is why they are the way that works.</string>
  <string key="temp_help_running">If one of them already runs and the reading is still missing, look at the ServerDashboard log on the server: it names every source it tried and the ones it found. Core Temp running on the desktop is not always visible to a dashboard running as a service: switch on "enable global shared memory" in Core Temp &gt; Options &gt; Advanced, or enable logging in Core Temp, so the dashboard can read its log file.</string>
